WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.379
PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE are related but

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:05.579
they are not the same thing The

00:00:05.606 --> 00:00:07.790
primary key is the table's official row

00:00:07.816 --> 00:00:10.885
identity and cannot be NULL A table

00:00:10.911 --> 00:00:13.556
has one primary-key constraint although that key

00:00:13.569 --> 00:00:18.178
may contain multiple columns UNIQUE protects other

00:00:18.191 --> 00:00:21.614
candidate keys or business rules A table

00:00:21.641 --> 00:00:26.676
can have several unique constraints PostgreSQL also

00:00:26.702 --> 00:00:29.044
has specific behavior around NULL values in

00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:31.176
unique constraints so do not assume a

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:33.716
UNIQUE column behaves exactly like a primary

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:37.960
key In the clinic example patient ID

00:00:37.973 --> 00:00:40.434
is the primary key while MRN remains

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.184
unique because duplicate medical record numbers
would

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.415
still be a serious data-quality problem A

00:00:47.441 --> 00:00:50.454
useful question is if the system-generated ID

00:00:50.468 --> 00:00:53.428
is already unique which business identifiers still

00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:54.915
need independent protection
